% ============================================================================
% wave-family-mechanism — for the two wave families, the underlying physical
% MECHANISM the SAME already-cited NASA sentence contrasts them by
% (adj-facts-stdlib, PHYSICS).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `wave-types.adj`
% (`wave_family(wave, family)`, 10 rows, which of the two families —
% mechanical or electromagnetic — a NAMED wave belongs to: water →
% mechanical, radio → electromagnetic, etc.). That table's own header
% already quotes, verbatim, a THIRD NASA sentence (beyond the two per-wave
% classification sentences already fully used to build all 10 rows) that
% states, in one continuous contrast, the defining MECHANISM of both
% families at once — a fact the per-WAVE key set had no room for:
%
%   "Light waves, also called electromagnetic waves, involve oscillations
%    of electric and magnetic fields rather than oscillations of matter."
%
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? wave_family_mechanism(electromagnetic, $Mechanism)
%       % oscillations_of_electric_and_magnetic_fields
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, an ordinary two-column EXACT
% lookup): each `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `wave_family_mechanism(family, mechanism)` carrying the citation, so the
% lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the
% mechanism WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls. Unlike its
% parent, this sibling is keyed by FAMILY (2 rows), not by individual WAVE
% (10 rows) — a coarser grain than `wave-types.adj`'s own key set, since
% the underlying fact (what a family's oscillation involves) is itself a
% per-family property, not a per-wave one. The query also runs BACKWARD —
% bind a mechanism and recall which family has it:
%
%     ? wave_family_mechanism($Family, oscillations_of_matter)
%       % mechanical
%
% A word that is NOT one of the two families — `radio`, `sound`, or any
% individual wave name — is deliberately NOT a row here (those belong to
% the parent's own per-wave key set), so a recall on one ABSTAINS rather
% than inventing a mechanism.
%
% Provenance: reproduces, byte-for-byte, the SAME NASA sentence already
% quoted inside `wave-types.adj`'s own header, from the SAME NASA Science
% James Webb Space Telescope education asset "Electromagnetic Wave (Light
% Wave) vs. Mechanical Wave" that table already cites — no new WebFetch,
% only a different schema decoding the mechanism half of the
% already-verified quote (the sentence states BOTH mechanisms at once, by
% contrast: electromagnetic waves involve oscillations of electric and
% magnetic fields "rather than" the oscillations of matter that mechanical
% waves involve). `trust authoritative` — the same tier `wave-types.adj`
% already carries (a primary U.S. government NASA education page).
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table wave_family_mechanism {
    columns family, mechanism

    row (electromagnetic, oscillations_of_electric_and_magnetic_fields)   % NASA: "Light waves, also called electromagnetic waves, involve oscillations of electric and magnetic fields rather than oscillations of matter."
    row (mechanical, oscillations_of_matter)                              % NASA: same sentence, contrasted mechanism — "...rather than oscillations of matter."

    source "Light waves, also called electromagnetic waves, involve oscillations of electric and magnetic fields rather than oscillations of matter."
    locator "https://science.nasa.gov/asset/webb/electromagnetic-wave-light-wave-vs-mechanical-wave/"
    trust authoritative
}
